   "source": [
    "## Step 2 — Prepare the Data\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why this matters:** You separate what the model learns FROM (features, X) from what it predicts (label, y). Then you hold back a test set the model never sees, so you can honestly judge it later.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Key concept — `stratify`:** because defaults are rare, a random split might accidentally put too few defaulters in your test set. `stratify=y` keeps the same default ratio in both train and test. Understand WHY this matters."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 35,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Full dataset default rate: 0.2212\n",
      "Train default rate: 0.22120833333333334\n",
      "Test default rate: 0.22116666666666668\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n",
    "\n",
    "# TODO: define X (all columns except 'default' and the 'ID' column) and y ('default')\n",
    "X = df.drop(columns=['default', 'ID'])\n",
    "y = df['default'] \n",
    "\n",
    "# TODO: do a stratified 80/20 train/test split\n",
    "X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(\n",
    "    X, y, test_size=0.2, stratify=y, random_state=42)\n",
    "\n",
    "# TODO: confirm the default rate is the same in y_train and y_test (proves stratify worked)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Full dataset default rate:\", y.mean())\n",
    "print(\"Train default rate:\", y_train.mean())\n",
    "print(\"Test default rate:\", y_test.mean())"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 3 — Train a Model\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why this matters:** Logistic regression is the INDUSTRY STANDARD baseline for credit scoring — Vojtech will know it intimately. Start simple and interpretable before anything fancy.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Key concept — `class_weight='balanced'`:** this tells the model to care more about the rare defaulters, countering the imbalance. Try it WITH and WITHOUT and compare in Step 4."
   ]
  },
